        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/silverleaf/{size}/2332_2.png",
        "created_at": "2015-04-12T00:59:41.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Craig\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/craig/48/2150_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Craig:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<div><b>cmschex wrote:</b><br><blockquote>I'm very fortunate that my wife embraced my passion for board games when I started getting into them a few years ago. &nbsp;In fact, almost every game I own has to play at least solidly two player because I still do most of my plays with her. &nbsp;In addittion to SotM (which we recently got my 8 yr old daughter hooked on), she really loves worker placement/euros - Keyflower, Viticulture and Castles of Burgundy are her favorites</blockquote></div>\nJealousy. I wish I could get Nick... or any friends, really... into the heavy, dry euros. \n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>My boyfriend doesn't go for those either. He prefers lighter stuff and things where you race cars while shooting each other, whereas I'm happy if there are cubes or meeples or victory points (preferably all three). We do have a fair bit of common ground though, so I can't complain really.</p>",
